  "aiOverview": {
    "content": "Flea-borne (murine) typhus is a bacterial disease caused by Rickettsia typhi and R. felis, transmitted to humans via infected flea feces, often from cats, rats, or opossums. Symptoms include high fever, headache, chills, and rash. While serious and potentially causing organ damage or requiring hospitalization, it is treatable with antibiotics. SymptomsSymptoms typically appear 6–14 days after exposure to infected flea feces, which can enter the body through cuts, scrapes, or being rubbed into the eyes. Common symptoms include: High fever Headache Chills Body aches and muscle pain Nausea and vomiting Rash (starts several days after initial symptoms) Cough Transmission and Causes Causative Agent: The disease is caused by the bacteria Rickettsia typhi or Rickettsia felis. Vector: The cat flea (Ctenocephalides felis) is the primary carrier in the United States. Transmission Mechanism: Infected fleas defecate while biting a human; the bacteria enters the bloodstream when the person scratches the bite area. It can also be transmitted by breathing in dust containing dried flea feces. Animal Hosts: Fleas often live on cats, rats, opossums, raccoons, skunks, and rodents. Diagnosis and Treatment Prognosis: Most cases are mild, but some patients can develop severe illness, including meningitis or organ damage. Deaths are rare (less than 1% of cases), but cases are often severe enough to require hospitalization. Treatment: Prompt treatment with antibiotics (specifically doxycycline) is effective. Diagnosis: Doctors often use blood tests (serology) and base diagnosis on clinical symptoms, especially if the patient is in a high-risk area. Prevention Pet Care: Use flea control products on pets regularly. Wildlife Management: Avoid feeding or approaching wild animals, particularly opossums and stray cats. Property Maintenance: Clear brush, debris, and pet food to reduce attracting wildlife. Areas AffectedFlea-borne typhus is found worldwide, especially in tropical and subtropical climates. In the United States, it is most common in Southern California, Hawaii, and Texas.",
